<h2>Project Specifications</h2>
<ul>
<li>Project type: Laboratory and R&amp;D campus</li>
<li>Location: Sunnyvale, California</li>
<li>Model type: Cutaway architectural presentation model</li>
<li>Scale: 1:200</li>
<li>Primary material: ABS</li>
<li>Key features: Exposed laboratory interior, representative equipment, perforated façade, site landscape and integrated lighting</li>
<li>Delivery: Coordinated transportation and on-site setup</li>
</ul>

<h2>Making the Laboratory Interior Visible</h2>
<p>The model needed to communicate a technically complex laboratory interior while preserving a clear understanding of the overall building and campus. To expose the laboratory floor, the slab above the selected interior area was omitted, creating a controlled cutaway view into the space.</p>
<p>At 1:200 scale, equipment, work areas, circulation routes, and human figures had to remain visually legible without overwhelming the architectural model. Interior elements were therefore simplified and represented at an appropriate level of detail while maintaining the organization and character of the laboratory environment.</p>

<h2>Translating the Perforated Façade to 1:200 Scale</h2>
<p>The building’s perforated façade contained openings that were too fine to reproduce literally at 1:200 scale. If reduced directly, many of the openings would become visually unclear or impractical to fabricate consistently.</p>
<p>Carve adjusted the opening size, spacing, and density to preserve the façade’s overall visual character at model scale. The resulting pattern communicates the intended transparency, rhythm, and surface texture while remaining durable and legible as a physical model.</p>
